<p>John Stuart Mill: The Greatest Happiness of the Greatest Number</p>
<p><img src="/phil2306/assets/images/360_f_805471673_dpduklb9i3s7xbtu9ekehwj5oaklds1n.jpg" alt="An illustration of a Victorian London street, evoking the social world of nineteenth-century England." width="860" height="340" loading="lazy" class="page-hero-image"></p>
<div class="callout callout--concept"><span class="callout__icon" aria-hidden="true">💡</span><div class="callout__content">
<p>Kant built morality on the rational structure of the will. Mill builds it on exactly what Kant rejected: the consequences of action, measured in human happiness.</p>
</div></div>
<div class="accordion">
<div class="accordion__item">
  <button class="accordion__header" aria-expanded="false" type="button">
    <span class="accordion__number">1</span>
    What this unit is about
    <span class="accordion__chevron" aria-hidden="true">▼</span>
  </button>
  <div class="accordion__content">
    <div class="accordion__body">
<p>Suppose someone asks you whether an action was right or wrong. You could answer in two very different ways. You could look at the action itself: was it done for the right reasons, did it respect the people affected, did it follow a principle anyone could accept? Or you could look at what the action <em>did</em>: did it make people's lives better or worse, did it reduce suffering, did it produce more good than the alternatives?</p>
<p>Most people, when they think hard about morality, find themselves drawn to both kinds of answer. But the two approaches sometimes conflict. A truthful answer might wound someone unnecessarily. A broken promise might prevent a disaster. When intentions and consequences pull in opposite directions, which one settles the moral question?</p>
<p>John Stuart Mill's answer is that consequences settle it, and that the right measure of consequences is human happiness. His <em>Utilitarianism</em>, published in 1861, is the most influential defense of this position ever written. Mill argues that there is a single moral principle, the <em>Greatest Happiness Principle</em>, that grounds every genuine moral claim: actions are right insofar as they tend to promote happiness, wrong insofar as they tend to produce its opposite. Everything else, duties and rights and virtues and justice, is supposed to reduce to applications of this single principle, properly understood.</p>
<p>This is a bold reduction, and Mill knows it. Much of the book is devoted to defending utilitarianism against objections, including the most natural one: that consequences cannot be the whole story, because some actions seem wrong (lying, killing the innocent, breaking one's word) even when they would produce more happiness than the alternative. Mill's response is that these intuitions are not mistaken but are themselves utilitarian, properly understood. We have moral rules against lying because lies typically produce more harm than honesty does. The rules track utility; they do not stand apart from it.</p>
<div class="callout callout--connection"><span class="callout__icon" aria-hidden="true">➡️</span><div class="callout__content">
<p>← LOOKING BACK &nbsp;|&nbsp; LOOKING AHEAD →</p>
<p>Kant argued that genuine moral principles must be categorical: binding regardless of what we want. Mill thinks this gets the order exactly backwards. We start with what creatures like us want (happiness, the absence of suffering), and we build moral principles from there. The Kant/Mill split is the central divide in modern ethics; understanding it well prepares you for everything that follows. Unit 5 brings Nietzsche, who will reject both projects: not just deontology and utilitarianism, but the entire idea that morality can be grounded in any single principle.</p>
</div></div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
<div class="accordion__item">
  <button class="accordion__header" aria-expanded="false" type="button">
    <span class="accordion__number">2</span>
    Before you begin
    <span class="accordion__chevron" aria-hidden="true">▼</span>
  </button>
  <div class="accordion__content">
    <div class="accordion__body">
<p>Mill wrote in English, and his prose is much more accessible than Kant's. You will find his sentences long, in the Victorian style, but his arguments are usually clear once you slow down enough to follow the clause structure. Unlike Kant, Mill is writing for the educated public rather than for fellow philosophers; he wants you to be persuaded, and he writes accordingly.</p>
<p>That said, the book is structured oddly by modern standards. Chapter 1 is a brief preface. Chapter 2 is the heart of the book: it tells you what utilitarianism is and answers most of the standard objections. Chapter 3 takes up a psychological question (what motivates people to be moral?). Chapter 4 offers Mill's notorious "proof" of the principle of utility, which has been controversial since the day it was published. Chapter 5, the longest, is on justice. Each chapter does something different, and the connections between them are not always obvious. The lecture pages will help you see the through-line.</p>
<div class="callout callout--concept"><span class="callout__icon" aria-hidden="true">💡</span><div class="callout__content">
<p>A WORD ON BENTHAM</p>
<p>Mill is constantly responding to Jeremy Bentham, the founder of modern utilitarianism and Mill's intellectual godfather. Bentham's version of utilitarianism was simpler: pleasure is good, pain is bad, and the right action is the one that maximizes the net surplus of pleasure over pain across everyone affected. Mill agrees with the basic frame but thinks Bentham missed some important distinctions, especially the distinction between higher and lower kinds of pleasure. Watch for moments where Mill is correcting Bentham without naming him; they are everywhere.</p>
</div></div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
