  <div class="accordion__content">
    <div class="accordion__body">
<p>By the end of this unit, you should be able to:</p>
<ul>
<li>State the Greatest Happiness Principle and explain what makes it a consequentialist principle.</li>
<li>Explain Mill's distinction between higher and lower pleasures and how it differs from Bentham's quantitative hedonism.</li>
<li>Describe the main objections to utilitarianism that Mill addresses in Chapter 2, and assess how well his replies succeed.</li>
<li>Distinguish external from internal sanctions and explain Mill's view of why people are motivated to act morally.</li>
<li>Reconstruct Mill's "proof" of the principle of utility and identify the move at which the proof is most commonly challenged.</li>
<li>Explain Mill's account of justice as a special class of utility, and assess whether his account succeeds in capturing what is distinctive about justice.</li>
<li>Distinguish act utilitarianism from rule utilitarianism and apply each to a concrete case.</li>
</ul>
