<p>All of our Mill reading comes from a single text: <em>Utilitarianism</em>, the short book Mill published in 1861. The full text runs about sixty pages, is in the public domain, and is free to read online.</p>
<ul>
<li><a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/files/11224/11224-h/11224-h.htm" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"><strong>Mill, <em>Utilitarianism</em></strong></a> (1861), five chapters</li>
</ul>
<p>The chapters are grouped into four parts, each paired with a lecture page in this unit.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Chapter 1</strong>: General Remarks (paired with the Greatest Happiness lecture)</li>
<li><strong>Chapter 2</strong>: What Utilitarianism Is (paired with the Greatest Happiness lecture)</li>
<li><strong>Chapters 3–4</strong>: Sanctions of the Principle, and Mill's Proof (paired with the Proof and Sanctions lecture)</li>
<li><strong>Chapter 5</strong>: Justice and Utility (paired with the Justice lecture)</li>
</ul>
